    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "How do leaders of allied states, dependent on the United States for security, shape foreign policy outcomes amidst pressures from the dominant ally and domestic actors? This thesis examines the impact of leadership preferences on foreign policy decision- making in South Korea and the Philippines, two key U.S. treaty allies in the Indo- Pacific. Drawing from interviews with over 40 foreign policy practitioners and detailed case studies, the research finds that allied state leaders’ preferences moderate the ef- fects of both international demands and domestic opposition. While U.S. pressure and domestic opinion establish limits to policy choices, leaders have individual policy pref- erences that determine how they navigate these constraints. Under leaders who sup- ported U.S. demands, the two nations strengthened their alliance with Washington. Conversely, those who favored a more independent foreign policy sought to balance U.S. interests with their own policy preference. In this process, leaders strategically aligned with domestic or U.S. demands, leveraging this alignment to justify their pre- ferred policy stance and override opposition from the other side. Finally, although leaders skeptical of U.S. demands eventually made concessions to preserve the alliance, the degree of accommodation varied based on their stance. These findings highlight the relevance of individual leadership in asymmetrical security partnerships, offering insights into the interplay between structural forces and individual factors in alliance management.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
